I've committed 68371ac6 (Part of #1202). A wildcard allow pattern like git *, cargo * or * now auto-approves a command only if the bash parser reads it as one plain command. Build, the full cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and fmt --check all pass.

What changed
• `src/bash_parse.rs`: new is_single_word_only_command. It says yes only when the parse has no errors, contains exactly one command, and every part is plain words, quoted strings or numbers. That copies Codex's word-only check, cut down to a single command. If the parse fails, the answer is no. The doc comment says the existing extract_check_units must never be used to prove a command safe.
• `src/config.rs`: one added line at the allow check calls a small helper that applies the new rule. The three old text checks are still there and the new one is added on top, so nothing that prompted before now auto-approves. The file is at 4684 of its 4696-line cap.
• `src/bash_parse_tests.rs` (new): the commands that must pass and must fail from the task, plus a before/after comparison over 100 (pattern, command) pairs.

Before/after comparison: none of the 100 pairs went from prompting to auto-approving. Four went from auto-approving to prompting:

| Pattern | Command | Why |
|---|---|---|
| git * | git log > out.txt | redirect |
| git * | git log $HOME | variable |
| git * | git status <<EOF… | here-doc |
| cargo * | cargo test 2>&1 | redirect |

cargo test 2>&1 asking under cargo * is on purpose: the redirect writes somewhere the pattern never named. Chains and command substitutions were already prompting under the old text check.

A newline inside double quotes is parsed as part of one string in one command, so it is approved. That's safe because there is no second command to run.

Where I departed from the plan: a wildcard pattern that spells out the chain or redirect itself (git * && ls, cargo * 2>&1) skips the new rule. Without that, an existing test I'm not allowed to delete (unchained_commands_are_still_auto_approved_byte_for_byte) would fail. The cost: git * && ls still approves git status && rm -rf x && ls, because the * covers the inner &&. That gap was already there; this change neither causes nor closes it. Matching each command in a chain separately is the next slice, and it's posted on #1202.

Checks
• Sabotage test: I made the new function always return true, with the NEUTERED marker counted before running. Three tests failed by name. After restoring it, all 12 passed and the marker count was 0.
• Effect on the evolve loop: none. .yoyo.toml has no [permissions] section, and evolve.sh pipes stdin to yoyo, which approves every tool call anyway.

Record: the flip table went onto #1202 before the commit (comment 6903299699). I then edited that comment once to correct a test name I had mistyped. The commit message carries the full record.

Docs: I did change docs/src/configuration/permissions.md, even though the plan said to avoid a fourth file. It still said "three rules" and that * matches "everything", which this change made wrong. It now has a section titled "A wildcard approves only one plain command".
